      body: `## A traveling apostle

Paul was constantly on the move. He wrote to the Romans that "from Jerusalem, and round about unto Illyricum, I have fully preached the gospel of Christ" (Romans 15:19), and listed among his sufferings: "in journeyings often, in perils of waters, in perils of robbers … thrice I suffered shipwreck, a night and a day I have been in the deep" (2 Corinthians 11:25–26). Acts organizes his travels into three "missionary journeys," followed by a voyage to Rome as a prisoner. The scheme is Luke's, and Paul himself never numbers his journeys; but it is a useful outline, and the map below follows it, with dates that are approximate.

## Before the journeys

After his call, Paul says, he went to "Arabia"—probably the Nabatean kingdom east and south of Damascus—and returned to Damascus. Three years later he went up to Jerusalem to meet Peter, staying fifteen days, and then went to "the regions of Syria and Cilicia" (Galatians 1:17–21). He recounts escaping from Damascus by being let down through a window in a basket, when the governor under the Nabatean king Aretas was trying to arrest him (2 Corinthians 11:32–33). Barnabas brought him to Antioch, and the church there became the base of his mission.

## The first journey

The church at **Antioch** sent out Barnabas and Paul, with Barnabas's cousin **John Mark** (Acts 13–14). They sailed to **Cyprus**, Barnabas's home, and crossed the island to **Paphos**, where the proconsul **Sergius Paulus** heard them. From this point Acts begins to call Saul by his Roman name, Paul. They then sailed to the southern coast of Asia Minor and went up into the highlands of **Galatia**, to Pisidian Antioch, Iconium, Lystra, and Derbe. At **Lystra**, after Paul healed a lame man, the crowds took Barnabas and Paul for the gods Zeus and Hermes and tried to offer sacrifice to them; soon afterward Paul was stoned and left for dead. The pattern of the journey recurs throughout Acts: Paul preaches first in the synagogue, gains some Jewish and many Gentile converts, meets opposition, and moves on.

![The journeys of Paul: the first journey and the meeting in Jerusalem](map:bib-110-journeys-of-paul#1-3 "Follow Paul and Barnabas out of Antioch, across Cyprus and up into Galatia, then back to Antioch and on to Jerusalem.")

## Into Europe

After the meeting in Jerusalem, Paul quarreled with Barnabas over John Mark, who had left the first journey early, and set out with **Silas** instead (Acts 15:36–41). Revisiting Galatia, he was joined by **Timothy**. At **Troas**, near ancient Troy, he had a vision of a man of Macedonia pleading, "Come over into Macedonia, and help us" (16:9). The crossing to **Neapolis** and **Philippi** brought the message to the European side of the Aegean. At Philippi, **Lydia**, a dealer in purple cloth, became the first convert; Paul and Silas were beaten and imprisoned. In **Thessalonica**, the capital of Macedonia, a riot drove them out. In **Athens**, Paul spoke before the council of the **Areopagus**, quoting Greek poets and proclaiming the "unknown god" (17:22–31). In **Corinth**, a great commercial city with two ports, he stayed for a year and a half, working with Priscilla and Aquila, and was brought before Gallio.

## Ephesus

On the third journey, Paul made **Ephesus**, the chief city of the province of Asia, his base for two to three years. This was the period of his most intense correspondence: 1 Corinthians was written from Ephesus, and probably Galatians, Philippians, and Philemon as well, if, as many scholars think, Paul was imprisoned there. Acts describes a riot led by the silversmith Demetrius, whose trade in silver shrines of the goddess **Artemis**, whose great temple was one of the wonders of the world, was threatened by Paul's preaching. The crowd filled the theater, shouting for two hours, "Great is Diana of the Ephesians" (19:34). Paul then traveled through Macedonia to Corinth, where he wrote his letter to the Romans, and turned toward Jerusalem with the collection.

## Prisoner to Rome

Paul's final journey to Jerusalem was full of foreboding; at Miletus he took leave of the elders of Ephesus, telling them they would see his face no more (20:17–38). In Jerusalem he was accused of bringing Gentiles into the Temple, attacked by a crowd, and rescued by the Roman garrison. He was held for two years in **Caesarea** under the governors Felix and Festus. As a Roman citizen, he appealed to Caesar, and was sent to Rome under guard. The voyage, told in vivid detail in Acts 27, ended in a storm of fourteen days and a shipwreck on **Malta**. In the spring the prisoners sailed on to Puteoli, and Paul went up the Appian Way to Rome.

![The journeys of Paul: Europe, Ephesus, and the voyage to Rome](map:bib-110-journeys-of-paul#4-7 "Watch the second journey cross from Troas into Europe, then follow Paul to Ephesus, back to Jerusalem, and across the sea to Malta and Rome.")

## What the journeys reveal

The pattern of Paul's travels reveals his strategy. He concentrated on **cities**, especially provincial capitals and ports on the main Roman roads and sea lanes: Thessalonica on the Via Egnatia, Corinth on the isthmus, Ephesus at the head of the road to the interior. He worked in his trade, met people in workshops and households, and left behind small communities that he continued to guide by letter and by sending co-workers like Timothy and Titus. The roads and sea lanes of the Roman peace, which we met in Lesson I, made his mission possible.

> **Pause and consider.** Why might Paul have concentrated on cities rather than the countryside? What advantages and dangers did urban centers offer to a new movement?`,
