      body: `## Real letters

Paul's letters are real letters, written to real people about real problems. They follow the conventions of ancient Greek letter-writing, adapted for his purposes. A typical letter opens with the names of the sender and recipients and a greeting—Paul transforms the usual Greek greeting *chairein* into *charis*, "grace," and adds the Jewish greeting "peace": "Grace be unto you, and peace, from God our Father, and the Lord Jesus Christ." A thanksgiving follows, often previewing the letter's themes; then the body; then ethical exhortation; then personal news and greetings, sometimes with a closing in Paul's own hand: "The salutation of me Paul with mine own hand" (1 Corinthians 16:21).

The letters were dictated to secretaries—one, Tertius, adds his own greeting in Romans 16:22—and carried by co-workers who would read them aloud to the community and perhaps explain them. They were written to be heard in an assembly, not read silently.

## 1 Thessalonians: the earliest letter

**1 Thessalonians**, written around 50 from Corinth, a few months after Paul left Thessalonica, is probably the oldest surviving Christian document. It is warm and personal. Paul recalls how the Thessalonians "turned to God from idols to serve the living and true God; And to wait for his Son from heaven" (1:9–10)—a summary that tells us they were Gentiles who had abandoned their gods. He had been forced to leave them quickly, and he sent Timothy back to encourage them.

The letter's most famous passage answers a pressing question. Some members of the community had died, and the others feared that the dead would miss the Lord's coming. Paul replies:

> For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air.
>
> — 1 Thessalonians 4:16–17 (KJV)

The word "we" shows that Paul expected to be alive at Christ's return. The early Christian expectation of an imminent end, which we met in the preaching of Jesus, is vivid here. (The modern idea of a "rapture" before a period of tribulation, built partly on this passage, was developed in the nineteenth century.)

## The Corinthian correspondence

Corinth was a Roman colony, refounded by Julius Caesar in 44 BCE after its destruction a century earlier, a bustling city of merchants, freedmen, and travelers. The church there gave Paul more trouble than any other. **1 Corinthians**, written from Ephesus around 54, responds to reports and to a letter the Corinthians had sent him, and treats an astonishing range of problems:

- **factions**, with some claiming to follow Paul, others Apollos or Cephas (1:12);
- a man living with his father's wife (5:1);
- believers taking one another to court before pagan judges (6:1–8);
- questions about marriage, celibacy, and divorce (7);
- whether believers may eat **meat sacrificed to idols** (8–10);
- women's head coverings in worship (11:2–16);
- abuses at the Lord's Supper (11:17–34);
- the use of **spiritual gifts**, especially tongues (12–14);
- doubts about the **resurrection of the dead** (15).

Underlying many of these problems, scholars think, was a sense among some Corinthians that they had already arrived spiritually: they were "wise," "spiritual," "full," and "reigning" (4:8). Paul responds with what he calls "the word of the cross": God chose "the foolish things of the world to confound the wise" (1:27). Against the claim that "all things are lawful," he insists that not all things build up the community. On meat offered to idols, he grants that "an idol is nothing in the world" but asks the "strong" to give up their rights for the sake of the "weak," whose conscience might be wounded.

**2 Corinthians** reveals a further crisis: rival missionaries, whom Paul sarcastically calls "super-apostles" (11:5), had come to Corinth, boasting of their credentials and visions, and questioning Paul's authority. Paul had made a "painful visit" and written a "tearful letter" (2:1–4). Many scholars think 2 Corinthians combines several letters, since its tone changes abruptly at chapter 10. In response to his critics, Paul boasts—ironically—of his weakness: his sufferings, his escape from Damascus in a basket, his "thorn in the flesh," and the Lord's answer to his prayer: "My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness" (12:9).

## Philippians and Philemon

**Philippians**, written from prison, is the most joyful of Paul's letters; the words "joy" and "rejoice" recur throughout. "Rejoice in the Lord alway: and again I say, Rejoice" (4:4). It contains the great hymn to Christ who "humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross" (2:5–11), which we will study later in this lesson.

**Philemon**, the shortest of Paul's letters, concerns a slave named **Onesimus** who had come to Paul, apparently after leaving his master Philemon. Paul sends him back, "Not now as a servant, but above a servant, a brother beloved" (16), and asks Philemon to receive him as he would receive Paul. We will return to this delicate letter when we consider Paul and society.

> **Pause and consider.** 1 Corinthians shows Paul applying his gospel to everyday questions: lawsuits, food, marriage, worship. What principles does he seem to use in deciding each one?`,
